      "question": {
        ":question/id": "4e84c628-87b9-5d74-b46c-f0558ebf65c9",
        ":question/problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=-1 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The third equation states that ${x}_{3}=3.$ Substitute this into the second equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-(3)=-1\\,\u27f9\\,{x}_{2}=-1+3=2.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=3$ and ${x}_{2}=2$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(2)+(3)=1\\,\u27f9\\,{x}_{1}=1-3+2=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=0$,  ${x}_{2}=2$, and ${x}_{3}=3.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $\u27e80,2,3\u27e9.$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
