      "question": {
        ":question/id": "ffbc1914-8f55-58fc-9939-7cf601faf78b",
        ":question/problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y-z &=2 \\\\ y-z &=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The third equation states that $z=3.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny-3=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\u27f9\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}+3=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $y=\\frac{7}{2}$ and $z=3$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx+\\frac{7}{2}-3=2\\,\u27f9\\,x=2-\\frac{7}{2}+3=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=\\frac{3}{2}$, $y=\\frac{7}{2}$, and $z=3.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{7}{2} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$",
